   "l": "Do the doing-without-forcing;\nattend to the no-affair;\ntaste the no-taste.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Act, but without forcing; make your business no business at all; taste what has no taste.",
   "n": "Three clauses cut to the same pattern: verb, then 無, then the same character again as a noun. 為為, 事事, 味味 — the first of each pair is the doing, the second is the thing done, and only the position tells you which. This is Classical Chinese at its barest: no inflection, no word-classes fixed in advance, the character standing in whatever job the slot gives it. You will meet the figure again at the end of chapter 64 (欲不欲, 學不學).\n\n無為 is bound here as one tap-unit because it is a term of the book, the thing this whole line is naming. 無事 and 無味 are not terms — they are struck off it on the spot, which is the joke.",
   "p": true
  },
