   "l": "Great, small; many, few —\nrequite a grudge with potency.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Great or small, many or few: pay back a grudge with potency.",
   "n": "大小多少 is the chapter's real crux, and it is four bare adjectives — no verb, no particle, no subject, nothing to tell you how they hang together. Three readings have been argued: treat the great as small and the many as few, that is, make little of what looks large; or make the small great and the few many, that is, repay a small kindness lavishly; or the four characters belong with the line before, continuing the list of things the sage does without. Some hold a line has simply dropped out. We leave the literal as bare as the Chinese is, because any smoothing of it is a choice the text does not make.\n\nWang Bi does not parse the four characters, but he does read them together with the line that follows, and he reads them of grudges: a small grudge is not worth paying back, a great one is what all under heaven already wants punished, and to go along with what all under heaven agrees on is 德, potency. On his reading the greats and smalls, the manys and fews, are sizes of injury.\n\n報怨以德 was famous early. The Analects has someone put it to Confucius — 'repay a grudge with potency, how is that?' — and gets back: then what will you repay potency with? Repay a grudge with straightness, and potency with potency."
