   ],
   "l": "Plan the difficult while it is still easy;\nwork at the great while it is still minute.\nAll-under-heaven's difficult affairs are bound to spring from the easy;\nall-under-heaven's great affairs are bound to spring from the minute.\nTherefore the sage to the very end does not make himself great,\nand so is able to accomplish his greatness.\nNow, giving assent lightly is bound to be scant in trust;\nmuch easy-taking is bound to mean much difficulty.\nTherefore the sage still treats things as difficult,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Lay your plans against the hard thing while it is still easy; work on the great thing while it is still small. Every hard business under heaven begins from an easy one; every great business under heaven begins from something minute. And so the sage never plays the great man, and that is how he accomplishes greatness. A promise given lightly is sure to be short on trust; taking much for easy is sure to make much hard. And so the sage treats even the easy as hard,",
   "n": "難 does four different jobs in this one unit: 'the difficult thing' as a noun, 'difficult' as a modifier before 事, 'difficulty' as an abstract noun, and finally a plain verb, 'to treat as difficult'. Nothing in the writing marks the change. Position does all of it, and this unit is as good a place as any to watch a Chinese character take four grammatical shapes without moving a stroke. We read all four nán; the last of them, the verb, is sometimes given the falling-tone reading nàn instead, the reading the character carries in the sense 'a calamity'.\n\n於其易 and 於其細: 其 points back to 難 and 大, the difficult and the great things just named — at their easy stage, at their minute stage.\n\n輕諾必寡信 is the only line in the chapter about speech and reads like a proverb quoted in, which it may well be.\n\nThe close is the pivot of the whole argument. It is not that the sage finds things hard; it is that he refuses to find them easy."
